    "case_id": "582",
    "case_summary": "A 71-year-old man presented with three weeks of jaundice, weight loss, abdominal pain, and dark-colored urine. He also reported bilateral lower-extremity weakness that was most noticeable when attempting to arise from a chair, resulting in several ground-level falls. He had no history of autoimmune or neuromuscular conditions. Despite his recent falls, he did not have any prolonged periods of being down that could contribute to muscle injury. He had been taking atorvastatin as a home medication.\n\nPhysical findings included:- Jaundice and significant scleral icterus- Epigastric tenderness without guarding or rebound- Mild weakness in bilateral hip flexor muscles\n\nLaboratory Results:- Aspartate aminotransferase: 2536 IU/L- Alanine aminotransferase: 919 IU/L  - Total bilirubin: 169.3 μmol/L- Alkaline phosphatase: 894 IU/L- Creatinine: 415.5 μmol/L (baseline of 70.7 μmol/L three months previously)- Initial creatinine kinase (CK) level: 22,000 IU/L, later peaked at >40,000 IU/L- Cancer antigen 19-9 level: 2930 kU/LImaging Studies:- CT abdomen/pelvis: 2.6 × 3.1 cm pancreatic head mass with associated pancreatic ductal dilatation, without evidence of metastatic disease- MRI: Diffuse myositis affecting lower extremities  Image Description: Axial and coronal views showing diffuse symmetrical hyperintense signal highly suggestive of diffuse myositisPathology:- Pancreatic head biopsy: Hypocellular with scant atypical cells- Biliary stricture biopsy: Consistent with adenocarcinoma- Right biceps femoris biopsy: Nonspecific myopathic changes consistent with rhabdomyolysis- BiliSeq genetic sequencing: Demonstrated genomic alterations in targeted regions of genes involved in biliary-tract malignancies",
